1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,480
Welcome to Episode 3 of Hello World, pg_bio!

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:06,960
If every cell in your body has the exact same DNA database, why is

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:10,440
a brain cell different from a liver cell?

4
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:13,920
The answer is Transcriptomics and allosteric regulation—which genes are turned on, and how distant

5
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:17,400
residues talk to each other.

6
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:22,230
When scientists run Single-Cell RNA sequencing on 1 million cells across 20,000 human genes,

7
00:00:22,230 --> 00:00:27,060
it creates a 20-billion-point matrix. Because a cell only expresses about 2,000 genes at

8
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:31,890
a time, over 90 percent of that matrix is zeroes.

9
00:00:31,890 --> 00:00:36,720
Storing dense columns in SQL or Pandas wastes gigabytes of RAM.

10
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:43,248
Systems engineers already know the cure: Compressed Sparse Row, or C-S-R.

11
00:00:43,248 --> 00:00:49,776
pg_bio implements a native SparseAttentionMap type using row pointers, column indices, and non-zero values.

12
00:00:49,776 --> 00:00:58,560
Whether you are unpacking single-cell RNA dosage, traversing ESM-2 transformer attention heads with get_top_interacting_residues,

13
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:07,344
or discovering cryptic allosteric pockets across 500-nanosecond molecular dynamics trajectories, CSR scans only the non-zero edges in microseconds.

14
00:01:07,344 --> 00:01:12,816
That slashes memory by 90 percent and runs 31 times faster than dense Python

15
00:01:12,816 --> 00:01:18,288
matrices. Read the full deep dive at ideia dot me and explore pg_bio on GitHub.
